#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}|}^{2} & ={|\\mathbf{a}|}^{2}+{|\\mathbf{b}|}^{2} \\\\  & ={10}^{2}+{20}^{2} \\\\  & =100+400 \\\\  & =500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :question/problem "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096968 :question/problem "![](images/a8/a89a9c2ff1cfd53a9d9e33c60591b482cbfa4044e313b2d2c031046bb21e5ce3.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{a}$?" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying by $3$ preserves direction and triples length. Multiplying by $-1$ reverses direction and preserves length. The correct diagram therefore has a long vector pointing northeast labeled $3a$, and a vector one-third as long pointing southwest labeled $-a$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096969 :question/problem "The vector $a$ has magnitude $5$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$." :question/worked-solution "Divide the vector by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\widehat a=\\frac{a}{|a|}=\\frac15a.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac15|a|=1$, and the positive scalar multiple has the same direction as $a$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x^5-4x^2$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=3u^2u^{\\prime}\\\\&=3(3x^5-4x^2)^2(15x^4-8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097030 :question/problem "Given that $4\\mathbf{a}-12\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Factor the first vector: $4a-12b=4(a-3b)$. Since this common vector is nonzero, equality with $\\lambda(a-3b)$ implies $\\lambda=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097031 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (-0.5\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})$ and $\\mathbf{a}-12\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "We have $a-12b=-2(-0.5a+6b)$. Since the vectors are nonzero, equality requires $\\lambda=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097032 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f54e51921dfd2fb1b93bccec89553e174e2527866352626a5bf0846956b096bb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $\\mathbf{a}$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector $-\\tfrac12a$ has half the length of $a$ and the opposite direction. The original vector points northeast, so the correct diagram shows a vector half as long pointing southwest."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097042 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $3$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $4$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal and vertical vectors are perpendicular, so\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n$$\n\nDividing by this magnitude gives the required unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}=\\frac15(a+b).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097043 :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 20$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 13$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "North-east and south-east directions are perpendicular. Hence\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{20^2+13^2}=\\sqrt{569}.\n$$\n\nThe unit vector in the direction of $a+b$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{\\sqrt{569}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097045 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $7$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "A unit vector in the direction of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ is obtained by dividing by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{|\\overrightarrow{AB}|}=\\frac17\\overrightarrow{AB}.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac17(7)=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097154 :question/problem "Differentiate $x^{2}\\cos x$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097158 :question/problem "![](images/28/285bc802c51b89c2dd5b64bd3ee5ff41bf49d798110fb0c9f29c59ea00f66cf8.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The grid gives b=(-4,-2), e=(2,1), c=(2,5), d=(-2,-5). Hence b=-2e and c=-d; b=2e is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097161 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dfaf74a497a2fd22428e09a4c26d573e184c9728ccf67f11ebb9d6a2a44f4c6.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $q + r$?" :question/worked-solution "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\langle4,\\frac32\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097162 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 5\\log_{3} x\\ln x$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}^{2}}{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}\\right)=\\frac{10 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097176 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c08ee3645692aa5630bc00d8dcd619fe4e6410b050afb6875339ab0962894ed2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The grid gives a=(6,4), c=(-3,-2), b=(-3,-5), d=(3,5). Thus c=-a/2; a=-c and b=d are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097177 :question/problem "![](images/13/1319298a994d5daf26853baf7d255490827ada4123debdf0b4374db1ed03f7af.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$." :question/worked-solution "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. The vector moves four grid squares right and seven down, so\n\n$$\nc=\\left\\langle2,-\\frac72\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad |c|=\\sqrt{2^2+\\left(-\\frac72\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{65}}2.\n$$\n\nDividing by the magnitude gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{|c|}=\\frac{2c}{\\sqrt{65}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097244 :question/problem "Differentiate $x^{2}\\tan x$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=x^{2} \\left(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right) + 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{-3}\\log_{2} x$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{3} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}} + \\frac{1}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097247 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y = 2e^{x}(x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x = 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x}\\right)=\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$." :question/worked-solution "With $u=x^4+3x$, we have $f=u^2$ and $u^{\\prime}=4x^3+3$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x-1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258 :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{4x - 3}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." :question/worked-solution "Write $y=(4x-3)^{1/2}$ and apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=\\frac12(4x-3)^{-1/2}\\cdot4=\\frac2{\\sqrt{4x-3}}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative is defined for $x>3/4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{′}=4x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (4x+3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y = 8x^{3}\\sin x$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x = \\frac{π}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=8 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 24 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n6 \\pi^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101207 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101208 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101210 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t - 2, y = t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From $x=t-2$ we have $t=x+2$. Substitution into $y=t^2$ gives $y=(x+2)^2$, with no restriction on real $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105457 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+x+{y}^{2})-x+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2(1+2{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
